\f1\fs22 Cracker was in misery. Since the news of her pregnancy, Sarah had dropped off the radar, keeping her head down, responding to no correspondence. He knew she did not want his ident on her comms records, but had not seen nor heard from Sarah and had lost track of how long it had been. Just over a week maybe? A week, without seeing her? Intolerable. He had more than once demanded the non-existent god to end his pain. He had completed a full 6 hour session staring at the wall, the very wall that she had also gazed upon, wallowing in self-pity, and thought to take his sorry carcass on a pub crawl. He never thought that drinking alone was a good idea and had no reason to think that this escapade would end well, but not even he could have guessed what would be the consequences of this little night on the turps.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You look like you have a lot on your mind. Do you want a couple of Elvi to listen? We are very good listeners.\'94 Elvis 2 said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sit down fellas and I\'92ll buy you both a drink.\'94 Cracker didn\'92t mind being taken for a bit of a ride if he could unload some misery. \'93Just don\'92t write a song about it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do you know about the Dawkins?\'94 Cracker giggled, the alcohol warming his body and dulling his head. \'93Let me tell you about the Dawkins. You\'92ll love this story.\'94 Cracker spoke and as he became more intoxicated, he became increasingly loose with his tongue and told them everything of how much he loved Sarah, the bombing, the disc, the strange men on the travelator and their \cb3 love of evolution, science and reason\cb1 . He told them of The Dawkins and of the expected birth of The Dawkins 2.0, the one-after-the-one. When he had unloaded fully, he was spent and said, \'93Praise be to the Dawkins.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\cb3 Tell me more about this evolution, man. It sounds great.\cb1 \'94 Elvis 1 said. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah. Evolution sounds like the real thing.\'94 Elvis 2 enthusiastically agreed. He had no choice but to agree with his doppelganger. Elvi not only looked the same, they thought the same. Exactly the same. Generally speaking, if you can convince one Elvis of something, you can convince them all. \'93I must know more of this Dawkins guy. He must have had it all together.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Wait a minute, I\'92ll get the others. They will want to hear about this.\'94 Elvis 1 proposed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He shortly returned with 4 more and by the end of the evening, Cracker had an audience of 20 Elvi transfixed by the evolution thing this Dawkins fellow had invented. Every Elvis that had worked in entertainment and hospitality, found it eminently plausible that their clients were related to monkeys. For the first time in his life Cracker was looked upon with admiration and it made him feel very uneasy. \cb3 They had broken out brain-dump caps and were downloading everything they could on evolution, science and reason\cb1 . The Chronicles of Dawkins etched into the brains of each Elvis before left he bar to go home. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please tell us of the blessed mother.\'94 Elvis 18 asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Virgin mother, actually.\'94 Cracker was truthful, but deliberately mischievous. It seemed he still had something of a sense of humour, even when he was feeling so retched.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Some with notepads out were already writing songs in praise of the great natural force of evolution, Charles Darwin, The Dawkins and the one to come. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have written a song. Let us sing the praises of the great one and the one-after-the-one.\'94 An Elvis proudly announced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An irate bar owner with a company of black clad security Elvi, waded through the crowd and stood before Cracker. \'93What are you doing? \cb3 You can\'92t teach these guys anything spiritual or philosophical. It voids my warranty! \cb1 Get the fuck out and never come back.\'94 At that the security Elvi picked up Cracker clean off his stool, carried him high above the crowd and out the door where they slammed him head first into the black stinking cement. The line of punters waiting to get inside, pointed and laughed heartily at Cracker, crumpled face first in the shit with his legs flapping about in the air. \cb3 The laughs turned silent as faces, painted pale in shock and fright, as they saw Cracker stand with the right side of neck bulging, white bones splitting the skin and his head lolling to the left. He straightened his head as best he could, ran a four-finger comb through his black hair and walked off with as much dignity as a shit-faced man with a busted neck could muster. \
\cb1 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He woke feeling no hangover and \cb3 his neck felt great when he moved his painless head in any and all directions. 
\f2\i \cb3 I could have sworn I was really hurt last night.
\f1\i0 \cb1  Then the memories of his chat with the Elvi popped to life in his head. At the time Cracker thought it pretty funny, but the dim morning light brought feelings of shame and guilt. \'93Oh dear.\'94 Cracker rolled his eyes. \'93What have I done?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The meme spread though out the Elvi and after only a few days polished, full orchestral renditions of new masterpieces were rolled out and became massive hits on the club scene and were soon being heard on audio platforms all over the world. No individual Elvis became rich, they had to share the royalties among their number, but collectively they became a powerful player in entertainment. Managers and agents were soon swimming in money as a brand new industry arose. The first song to hit the big time was a beautiful ballad of sincere love for Darwin, science and evolution. There was rarely a dry eye in the house when played live.\
